# Normalise line endings for every text file Git can detect one in: LF in the
# repository, whatever the platform wants on checkout. Without this the policy
# lives in each clone's core.autocrlf, so a contributor with it unset can commit
# CRLF and the file then reads as entirely changed for everybody else.
#
# It does not stop a local tool writing LF into a working file - only a checkout
# puts that right - so a scripted edit still wants checking afterwards.
#
# The eol=lf pins below deliberately come after this line: later rules win.
* text=auto

# Dart Sass emits LF line endings. Keep the generated CSS as LF so Git does not
# rewrite it to CRLF (which caused "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings and
# noisy diffs on every recompile).
ODK.Web.Razor/wwwroot/css/*.css text eol=lf

# esbuild emits LF, and build-bundles.mjs normalises its inputs to LF so the one
# bundle it does not minify is deterministic too. Pinned for the same reason as the
# CSS above - the generated CSS rule already covers css/odk.bundle.lib.css.
ODK.Web.Razor/wwwroot/js/odk.bundle*.js text eol=lf

# Git hooks are shell scripts run via /bin/sh - keep them LF so the shebang isn't
# broken by CRLF on Windows checkouts.
.githooks/** text eol=lf
